export default "## What \"anonymous tracking\" actually means here\n\nTrue anonymity is a spectrum, not a binary. This guide covers the practical steps to minimize the data trail from Floriva, which is already small by design, without overstating what is achievable.\n\nWhat you can control with these steps:\n- Whether any data leaves your device through Floriva's infrastructure (you can keep sync off)\n- Whether health platform integrations route your data through Apple or Google ecosystems\n- Whether device backups include app data accessible on a shared account\n- Whether someone with physical access to your phone can open the app\n\nWhat you cannot control through app settings:\n- Whether the App Store or Google Play logged that you downloaded a period tracking app (they do, against your account)\n- Whether your device's operating system logs that the app was opened\n\nMost users are concerned about data brokers and app companies, intimate partner surveillance, or legal requests. These steps address the first two significantly and the third as much as app settings allow.\n\n## The default Floriva setup is already more private than most apps\n\nFloriva keeps core data on your device and requires no account for core tracking. That gives it a smaller exposure surface than cloud-connected trackers before you change any settings. Flo, Clue, and similar apps store data on servers by default. Flo's 2021 FTC enforcement action documented data sharing with Facebook and Google. Optional Floriva sync sends only end-to-end encrypted data that Floriva cannot read.\n\nThe steps below address the remaining surface area.\n\n## Step-by-step anonymous setup\n\n### No account, no name\n\nFloriva does not require an account. This is the most important setting: there is no account to require. When you open the app for the first time, skip any prompts that suggest creating an optional account for backup or sync features.\n\nIf the app asks for a name during onboarding, enter whatever you like. The name is for app use. It does not create a user profile for core tracking.\n\n### Health platform connections\n\nApple Health and Google Fit are platform-level health data aggregators. Connecting Floriva to either of them routes your cycle data through Apple's or Google's data infrastructure, which has its own data handling terms and can be accessed by apps with health platform permissions.\n\nFor anonymous tracking, keep Floriva isolated. Do not accept the prompt to connect to a health platform. If you have already connected it, revoke the permission: on iOS in Settings, Health, Apps, Floriva; on Android in Settings, Google Fit or Health Connect.\n\n### Cloud backup exclusion\n\nDevice backups are a frequently overlooked exposure point. If your phone backs up to iCloud or Google Drive, and your backup account is shared with or accessible to someone else, they could access backup data including Floriva's local app data.\n\nThe specific paths for disabling this are in step 4 of the setup guide above. Check whether iCloud Backup or Google Backup is enabled for the device, and if so, verify that Floriva is excluded from what it syncs.\n\n### Lock screen and notification hygiene\n\nYour device's lock screen is the primary barrier between your Floriva data and anyone with your device. Use a passcode or biometric authentication. Do not use a pattern unlock, which can be observed.\n\nFloriva's push notifications may display cycle reminders or tracking prompts on your lock screen. If you share a home with others or use your device in shared spaces, review your notification privacy settings. On iOS: Settings, Notifications, Floriva, Show Previews, When Unlocked. This prevents notification content from appearing on the lock screen.\n\n## What this setup protects against\n\nAfter completing these steps, your Floriva setup:\n\n- Stores no readable cycle records on Floriva servers\n- Creates no account linkable to your identity\n- Routes no data through Apple Health or Google Fit ecosystems\n- Excludes app data from device cloud backup\n- Requires device authentication to view\n\nThe remaining exposure is physical: someone with access to your unlocked device can open the app. An additional app lock (step 5 above) addresses this without requiring a Floriva-specific feature.\n\nFor the complete picture of how period trackers handle data and what legal access looks like, see our guides on [how period tracker apps collect data](/resources/guides/how-period-tracker-apps-collect-data) and [whether police can access period tracker data](/resources/guides/can-police-access-period-tracker-data).";
